{
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "What's the general feel of the city above ground?",
      "type": "single_select",
      "options": [
        "Near-future megacity, slightly dystopian",
        "Contemporary realist, like a real city today",
        "Post-collapse, scrappy and rebuilding",
        "Retro-futurist, alternate history vibes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "How did the transit network get abandoned in the first place?",
      "type": "single_select",
      "options": [
        "Financial collapse or political failure",
        "A disaster (flood, war, plague, accident)",
        "Corporate or government cover-up of something that happened down there",
        "It was never finished \u2014 construction just stopped"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Who controls access to the tunnels now?",
      "type": "multi_select",
      "options": [
        "Organized criminal factions",
        "A grassroots community that self-governs",
        "A rogue city agency or ex-transit workers",
        "Nobody \u2014 it's a free-for-all",
        "A private corporation that bought the infrastructure"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "What's the primary reason ordinary people use the underground network?",
      "type": "multi_select",
      "options": [
        "It's faster than the gridlocked surface",
        "It's free, and surface transit is expensive",
        "Privacy \u2014 no surveillance cameras down there",
        "Certain goods or services only exist underground",
        "To avoid checkpoints, borders, or enforcement zones above"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "What's the biggest danger below ground?",
      "type": "multi_select",
      "options": [
        "Environmental (flooding, gas, structural collapse)",
        "Human (gangs, enforcers, predatory operators)",
        "Something stranger \u2014 creatures, contamination, unexplained phenomena",
        "The network itself \u2014 old systems that still activate unpredictably"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "What's the tone and genre you want for stories set here?",
      "type": "single_select",
      "options": [
        "Thriller or heist \u2014 tense, propulsive, high stakes",
        "Political drama \u2014 factions, power, moral compromise",
        "Adventure with heart \u2014 ensemble cast, camaraderie, hope",
        "Mystery or noir \u2014 secrets buried deep, literally and figuratively"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "What was the disaster that closed the tunnels, and roughly when did it happen relative to the story's present day?",
      "options": [
        "A catastrophic flood decades ago \u2014 the tunnels were drowned and sealed",
        "A plague outbreak that originated underground \u2014 people fled and never came back",
        "A war or civil conflict that used the tunnels as battlegrounds",
        "An industrial accident (gas, fire, collapse) that the city covered up"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "What's the retro-futurist flavor of the world above? This shapes architecture, technology, and politics.",
      "options": [
        "1940s-50s atomic age optimism \u2014 art deco towers, clean energy, Cold War tension",
        "1960s space-race idealism \u2014 sleek modernism, orbital ambitions, social upheaval",
        "Diesel-punk industrial \u2014 heavy machinery, unions, smokestacks, gritty grandeur",
        "Biopunk organic \u2014 bioengineered materials, living architecture, natural-tech fusion"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "What kind of 'strange' exists in the tunnels? This is the world's most delicious mystery.",
      "options": [
        "Bioluminescent organisms and mutated wildlife \u2014 nature reclaimed the dark",
        "Psychic or perceptual anomalies \u2014 people hear things, see things, lose time",
        "A contamination that changes people who spend too much time below",
        "Something was built down there that nobody talks about \u2014 and it still runs"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "What era does Veldrun's retro-futurism peak at aesthetically? This shapes how each district looks and what its architecture signals about its status.",
      "options": [
        "Early 60s \u2014 Kennedy-era optimism, clean lines, civic pride on every facade",
        "Mid 60s \u2014 space race fever, bold geometry, everything reaching skyward",
        "Late 60s \u2014 starting to crack, protest murals next to gleaming towers, tension visible"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "How does the city's surveillance system work above ground? This shapes what the tunnels mean to each district differently.",
      "options": [
        "ID card checkpoints at transit nodes \u2014 you can't ride the surface rail without scanning",
        "Camera networks with facial recognition \u2014 no checkpoints, but you're always watched",
        "A points-based civic score \u2014 infractions reduce access to services and jobs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Which district do you want to be the emotional heart of the story \u2014 the one whose people and stakes feel most central?",
      "options": [
        "Ironbell \u2014 the working class district that lost the most in the Pocket Fire",
        "The Margin \u2014 migrants and outsiders who need the tunnels most urgently",
        "Tessmer Heights \u2014 where the truth about the compound might finally surface",
        "The Meridian \u2014 where the cover-up lives and the powerful make their choices"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "How scientifically grounded do you want the biology to feel? This sets the tone for how characters talk about and understand what they find.",
      "options": [
        "Hard science \u2014 everything has a plausible real-world biological mechanism, even if strange",
        "Soft science \u2014 biological logic, but with room for things that defy easy explanation",
        "Mythic \u2014 the ecology operates on its own internal rules that characters can learn but not fully explain"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "The compound from the geological cavity is the origin of all the strangeness. What do you want its nature to ultimately be?",
      "options": [
        "A natural substance \u2014 ancient, pre-human, with no agenda",
        "A byproduct of something the city built or tested underground before the official boring began",
        "Genuinely unknown \u2014 even by the end of the story, its origin remains open"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "The deep water in the flooded sub-basement is the biggest unknown. How strange do you want what lives there to be?",
      "options": [
        "Strange but biological \u2014 something that evolved in isolation, like a cave ecosystem taken further",
        "Liminal \u2014 it may be biological, may be something else, the characters can never quite confirm",
        "Leave it unrevealed \u2014 the deep water stays off the page, its power coming entirely from what characters fear it might be"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "The Keeper-General council has seven members. What's the internal dynamic that creates the most interesting friction?",
      "options": [
        "A founding generation vs. a younger generation with different values",
        "True believers in the tunnels as sanctuary vs. pragmatists who'd cut deals with the city",
        "People united by the Fire's legacy vs. people who just need the network to survive",
        "A quiet power struggle between two Keeper-Generals with competing visions"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "Who is the protagonist-adjacent Steward \u2014 the one a story would most naturally follow?",
      "options": [
        "A Line Warden who knows the tunnels better than anyone and is starting to change physically",
        "A Lamplighter who has figured out more about the Bloom than anyone should know",
        "A young Keeper-General inheriting their parent's seat after a death",
        "A voucher \u2014 someone who brings people in \u2014 who let the wrong person through"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the Stewards' greatest unresolved internal wound \u2014 the thing they haven't dealt with that will eventually force a reckoning?",
      "options": [
        "They covered up the first compound persistence cases to protect the network, and the affected people never consented to the silence",
        "A Keeper-General made a deal with a city official years ago that nobody else knows about",
        "Someone was banned from the tunnels for a rules violation and later died above ground in circumstances that feel connected",
        "They've known about the broker selling access for longer than they've admitted and didn't act fast enough"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "question": "Every great fictional city has a specific geography that shapes its politics and culture. What's Veldrun's dominant physical feature?",
      "options": [
        "A bay or harbor \u2014 the city wraps around water, with wealth on the high ground and workers at the waterfront",
        "A river bisecting the city \u2014 two halves with different histories that have never fully unified",
        "A ridge or escarpment \u2014 one side faces the sea, one faces inland, and the divide is cultural as much as physical",
        "A crater or depression at the center \u2014 the city grew outward from an industrial heart that still defines everything"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "What is Veldrun's relationship to the broader world? This shapes who comes here, why, and what the city thinks of itself.",
      "options": [
        "A regional capital \u2014 powerful within its territory, largely self-referential, suspicious of outside influence",
        "A port city in decline \u2014 once a major trade hub, now watching younger cities absorb its relevance",
        "An ideological showcase \u2014 built or rebuilt to demonstrate something, still performing that demonstration",
        "An isolated city-state \u2014 surrounded by different political territory, dependent on its own resources"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    },
    {
      "question": "What does Veldrun's government actually look like? The surveillance grid exists, but who runs it and how do they justify it?",
      "options": [
        "An elected municipal council that has gradually expanded its own authority over decades, each expansion justified by a crisis",
        "A technocratic administration \u2014 unelected specialists who run the city as an engineering problem and believe this is neutral",
        "A coalition government perpetually on the verge of collapse, held together by the one thing all factions agree on: controlling information",
        "A single dominant party with genuine popular support that has held power long enough to forget the difference between the party and the city"
      ],
      "type": "single_select"
    }
  ]
}
